diff --git a/docs/dev/TechnicalDebt.md b/docs/dev/TechnicalDebt.md
--- a/docs/dev/TechnicalDebt.md
+++ b/docs/dev/TechnicalDebt.md
@@ -397,3 +397,5 @@
 - [sceVideodec2CreateDecoder](../../core/libs/prx/libSceVideodec2/Export.cpp) (libSceVideodec2) decodes HEVC (codec type 974921) and VP9 (2382845) through FFmpeg, besides H.264. The tags come from SharpProspero's `Videodec2CodecType` and EVO-PLAYER-PS5's `sce_videodec2.h`, which records them from hardware runs. HEVC input is converted from length-prefixed NAL units to Annex B as for H.264, and VP9 frames are passed through. Output is the same 8-bit NV12 for every codec; a decoder for HEVC Main10 or VP9 Profile 2 is created, but a frame deeper than 8 bits throws, since the console's 10-bit output layout is unknown, and `QueryDecoderMemoryInfo` reports the 8-bit frame size for it. `sceVideodec2GetPictureInfo` and `sceVideodec2GetAvcPictureInfo` throw for HEVC and VP9 pictures, which use other layouts (`SceVideodec2HevcPictureInfo`, `SceVideodec2Vp9PictureInfo`), and `sceVideodec2GetHevcPictureInfo` and `sceVideodec2GetVp9PictureInfo` are not exported
 - [syslog](../../core/libs/prx/libc/src/Syslog.cpp) (libc) writes each message to `stderr` as `[syslog:<priority>] <message>`, with a newline added when missing, instead of sending it to the console's system log. Priority and facility handling follow FreeBSD's `vsyslog`: `%m` expands to the guest errno's message, unknown bits are reported (as priority 0x23, since FreeBSD passes `LOG_ERR|LOG_CONS|LOG_PERROR|LOG_PID` as the priority) and masked, a missing facility becomes `LOG_USER`, and errno is preserved. `openlog`, `setlogmask` and `closelog` are not implemented, so the identity, options and mask they set are not applied
 - [sigaction](../../core/libs/prx/libkernel/System/src/Signals.cpp) (libkernel) records the handler, flags and mask of every signal from 1 to 128 and reports them back, following FreeBSD's `sigaction` (`EINVAL` for other signals, and for a handler on `SIGKILL` or `SIGSTOP`); `signal` records its handler with `SA_RESTART`, as FreeBSD's libc does. Only handlers without `SA_SIGINFO` on the signals `signal` already delivers (`SIGINT`, `SIGILL`, `SIGABRT`, `SIGFPE`, `SIGSEGV`, `SIGTERM`) run, the same way `signal` handlers do; their mask and flags are not applied. `SA_SIGINFO` handlers and other signals (`SIGBUS`, `SIGHUP`, ...) are recorded but never called, since that needs FreeBSD's `siginfo_t` and `ucontext_t`; `raise` of an `SA_SIGINFO` handler on a signal `signal` delivers throws instead of terminating, and `raise` of the other signals still returns `EINVAL`
+
+- Typed image placeholders (PR #2015 with local policy integration) are real 1x1 zero-initialized images, distinct from Vulkan native-null descriptors. Sampling and fetches observe the chosen heap format, valid extent and fixed sample count (4 for MS heaps); image size/sample queries, comparison sampling, out-of-range access, storage writes and atomics do not acquire native-null semantics. Storage descriptors in a heap alias its placeholder and writes may survive later draws. A missing format, sample, portability-array or atomic feature leaves that heap unavailable and its first null use fails explicitly. `RequiresValidImages` continues to reject zero guest descriptors and zero Vulkan views; `NativeNull` still requires the actually enabled robustness2 feature. `TypedPlaceholders` has a separate request15/cache22 identity. No bare zero Vulkan image view is used in the fallback.
